---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import '../../styles/lab.css';
---
<SiteLayout title="Research — Worldeology Lab" description="Four connected research programs for Cognitive AI: cognition, learning, world engines, and intelligence architecture." headerTone="paper" active="research" image="/og.png">
  <main id="main" class="lab-site lab-page research-page">
    <header class="lab-page-hero research-page-hero">
      <p class="lab-index">Worldeology Lab / Research</p>
      <h1>Research</h1>
      <p>Four connected programs follow intelligence from unspoken intent to learning, world models, and architecture.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="lab-page-section research-index-section" aria-labelledby="program-title">
      <header class="research-index-header">
        <p id="program-title" class="lab-index">Research fields</p>
        <p>Select a direction to read its definition and related work.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="research-explorer">
        <div class="research-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Research fields">
          <button id="tab-cognitive-ai" class="program-cognitive" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="cognitive-ai" data-program="cognitive-ai"><strong>Cognitive AI</strong></button>
          <button id="tab-learning-from-cognition" class="program-learning" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="learning-from-cognition" data-program="learning-from-cognition" tabindex="-1"><strong>Learning from Cognition</strong></button>
          <button id="tab-engine-of-world" class="program-world" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="engine-of-world" data-program="engine-of-world" tabindex="-1"><strong>Engine of World</strong></button>
          <button id="tab-architecture-of-intelligence" class="program-architecture" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="architecture-of-intelligence" data-program="architecture-of-intelligence" tabindex="-1"><strong>Architecture of Intelligence</strong></button>
        </div>

        <div class="research-panels">
          <article id="cognitive-ai" class="research-panel program-cognitive" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-cognitive-ai">
            <div class="program-essay">
              <h2>Cognitive AI</h2>
              <p class="program-lead">We have spent a long time teaching machines to answer questions, or to carry out tasks.</p>
              <p>A larger question has only just surfaced: what did the machine understand when it received an instruction? As reasoning took shape, could it notice its own deviation? And once an output left the screen and entered a person’s life, where did it push the world?</p>
              <p>Language and scenes are only the part of cognition that briefly rises above the surface. Below it lie guesses about another person’s intentions, plans that weigh competing interests, and countless reasoning paths converging under uncertainty. Beyond the surface, action participates in reality. It changes a decision, a relationship, even a future path that otherwise would never have existed.</p>
              <p class="program-thesis">Cognitive AI studies this entire process.</p>
              <p>It expands the basic unit of intelligence from a single output to a cognitive trajectory: understanding minds before action, examining cognition during action, tracing and bearing the consequences after action, and continuing to learn and generalize across events. Future AI will not remain inside a prompt box forever, passively waiting for a question and returning an answer. It will enter sustained collaboration, education, companionship, and decision-making. Then, the correctness of a single response will no longer be enough. A system must know whom it is facing, how a judgment is taking shape, and what its own action will leave behind over time.</p>
              <p><strong>Before the answer: understand another mind.</strong> Human interaction is never transparent. People hint, withhold, and test. Their real intentions may hide behind an ordinary action. Before it speaks, Cognitive AI confronts these invisible states—inferring from context, relationships, and behavior what another person may believe, what they may need, and which boundaries must not be crossed.</p>
              <p><strong>Inside the answer: observe and examine cognition.</strong> Cognitive AI turns generation from a line that can only move forward into a path that can be seen, inspected, and retraced. When reasoning begins to drift, the system need not wait until the error has been fully stated and then explain it. It can find where the deviation began, return, and choose again.</p>
              <p><strong>After the answer: model how action changes the world.</strong> Once an action enters reality, it is no longer merely a physical interaction. A moment of comfort may change another person’s beliefs; one suggestion may initiate a major event. Every step can appear reasonable while the long-term outcome slowly moves in the opposite direction. Cognitive AI is concerned with the world that continues to grow after an action.</p>
              <p>We intend to establish a new field: an AI research program that looks not only at the surface of an answer, but also at the cognitive process from which it emerged and the trajectory it leaves after passing through reality. Understanding, examination, and consequence thus become objects that can be modeled, trained, evaluated, and optimized.</p>
              <p class="program-closing">The answer is a point in the age of AI. Cognition begins here—and continues into the future.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="program-work-list" aria-label="Cognitive AI related work"><p>Related work</p><a href="/blog/MetaMind/technical-contribution/"><span>MetaMind</span><small>Modeling unspoken minds</small><i>↗</i></a><a href="/blog/MetaMind/cognitive-frontier/"><span>Cognition-of-Thought</span><small>Reflective and revisable cognition</small><i>↗</i></a><a href="/projects/mariolm/"><span>MarioLM</span><small>Consequential interactive worlds</small><i>↗</i></a></div>
          </article>

          <article id="learning-from-cognition" class="research-panel program-learning" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-learning-from-cognition" hidden>
            <div class="program-essay"><h2>Learning from Cognition</h2><p class="program-lead">Turn cognitive experience into persistent capability.</p><p>This program studies how reflection, prediction error, memory, and test-time evidence become reusable knowledge rather than another stored transcript. The learning signal is the evolving cognitive process, not only the final answer.</p></div>
            <div class="program-work-list" aria-label="Learning from Cognition related work"><p>Related work</p><a href="/blog/MetaMind/cognitive-frontier/"><span>Cognition-of-Thought</span><small>Learning from reflective reasoning traces</small><i>↗</i></a><a href="/blog/MetaMind/technical-contribution/"><span>MetaMind</span><small>Structured hypotheses about latent minds</small><i>↗</i></a></div>
          </article>

          <article id="engine-of-world" class="research-panel program-world" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-engine-of-world" hidden>
            <div class="program-essay"><h2>Engine of World</h2><p class="program-lead">Model the world as a living causal system.</p><p>An interactive world is more than a visual scene. It contains latent state, causal event chains, relationships, durable memory, and future branches that let an agent reason across a full horizon.</p></div>
            <div class="program-work-list" aria-label="Engine of World related work"><p>Related work</p><a href="/projects/mariolm/"><span>MarioLM</span><small>Persistent, consequential interactive worlds</small><i>↗</i></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03999"><span>Long-Horizon Deception</span><small>Safety across extended interaction</small><i>↗</i></a></div>
          </article>

          <article id="architecture-of-intelligence" class="research-panel program-architecture" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-architecture-of-intelligence" hidden>
            <div class="program-essay"><h2>Architecture of Intelligence</h2><p class="program-lead">Redesign the mechanisms that intelligence reasons through.</p><p>We study semantic, knowledge, and structural bottlenecks in current models, then build inference-time and learning architectures that can inspect, control, and revise their own cognitive boundary.</p></div>
            <div class="program-work-list" aria-label="Architecture of Intelligence related work"><p>Related work</p><a href="/blog/MetaMind/cognitive-frontier/"><span>Cognition-of-Thought</span><small>Process-level cognitive control</small><i>↗</i></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.21906"><span>Confident Decoding</span><small>Inference-time model mechanisms</small><i>↗</i></a></div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-page-section" aria-labelledby="dimensions-title">
      <header><p class="lab-index">02 / Cognitive dimensions</p><h2 id="dimensions-title">What a cognitive system must eventually support.</h2></header>
      <div class="lab-principles">
        <article><b>01</b><h3>Long / full horizon</h3><p>Maintain resolution, attention, and causal structure across extended language and event trajectories.</p></article>
        <article><b>02</b><h3>Personalization</h3><p>Adapt interpretation and action to the person, context, and evolving relationship.</p></article>
        <article><b>03</b><h3>Subjectivity</h3><p>Represent preferences, aesthetics, social meaning, and uncertain latent states without reducing them to a single rigid score.</p></article>
        <article><b>04</b><h3>Agency</h3><p>Actively discover missing information, ask when needed, and choose a useful reasoning strategy.</p></article>
        <article><b>05</b><h3>Generalization</h3><p>Carry abstractions beyond memorized patterns into new people, tasks, and worlds.</p></article>
        <article><b>06</b><h3>Creativity</h3><p>Produce new cognitive patterns and hypotheses rather than only recombining familiar answers.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <SiteFooter />
  </main>
  <script>
    const tabs = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-program]')];
    const panels = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.research-panel')];

    const activateProgram = (programId: string, updateHash = false) => {
      const activeTab = tabs.find((tab) => tab.dataset.program === programId);
      if (!activeTab) return;
      tabs.forEach((tab) => {
        const isActive = tab === activeTab;
        tab.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(isActive));
        tab.tabIndex = isActive ? 0 : -1;
      });
      panels.forEach((panel) => { panel.hidden = panel.id !== programId; });
      if (updateHash) history.replaceState(null, '', `#${programId}`);
    };

    tabs.forEach((tab, index) => {
      tab.addEventListener('click', () => activateProgram(tab.dataset.program ?? '', true));
      tab.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
        if (!['ArrowDown', 'ArrowUp', 'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight'].includes(event.key)) return;
        event.preventDefault();
        const nextIndex = ['ArrowDown', 'ArrowRight'].includes(event.key) ? (index + 1) % tabs.length : (index - 1 + tabs.length) % tabs.length;
        tabs[nextIndex].focus();
        activateProgram(tabs[nextIndex].dataset.program ?? '', true);
      });
    });

    const openFromHash = () => activateProgram(location.hash.slice(1) || 'cognitive-ai');
    openFromHash();
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', openFromHash);
  </script>
</SiteLayout>
